  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints `27.06` (it was printing `26.09`).\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"12.5\"` became `\"12.05\"` and was read as 1205 cents instead of 1250. The two items then came to 2410 cents, and 2410 + 199 tax = 26.09. The fix is `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, which gives 2500 + 206 tax = 2706, or 27.06. Prices with two decimals, like `12.05`, were already right, so only one-decimal prices were wrong.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran `python3 -m unittest`. All 7 tests pass. That's the 6 existing ones, which never called `to_cents` directly, which is why they missed this, plus a new regression test in `tests/test_money.py`.\n\n**Another bug, not fixed:** `to_cents` also gets negative amounts wrong. `\"-1.50\"` comes out as -100 + 50 = -50 instead of -150. The quote command doesn't take negative prices, so this didn't affect your case. Say if you want it fixed too.",
